patches/0021 -- ath12k: a16_power_cycle, treat a system suspend as a power cycle

Why
---
Upstream documents that s2idle can power devices down on these Qualcomm SoCs:

    [RFC PATCH 0/1] soc: qcom: rpmh-rsc: Register s2idle_ops to indicate s2ram behavior in s2idle

      "the quirky platform behavior of mimicking s2ram in s2idle for older Qcom SoCs ... device
       drivers ... need to prepare for the possible power loss during system suspend by shutting
       down or resetting the devices."

Measured here on 2026-10-02 (boot 01bd23bd, evidence in
BRINGUP/evidence/2026-10-02-s2idle-keepmhi0-radio-still-dies-usb-too.txt): after a suspend the WiFi
device is healthy at the PCIe level -- link 8.0 GT/s x1, power_state D0, driver bound, `wlP4p1s0`
present -- while its firmware is dead (`ath12k_wifi7_pci 0004:01:00.0: failed to resume core: -110`),
and a USB controller fails the same way in the same resume pass
(`dwc3-qcom a600000.usb: PM: failed to resume: error -110`).  The earlier A16 resume work assumed the
opposite -- keep the MHI link, or keep the device with `mhi_power_down_keep_dev()` -- which is why the
radio kept dying.  This patch makes the driver behave as if the device lost power, because on this
machine it may have.

What it adds
------------
`a16_power_cycle` (bool, default 1, 0644 -- writable at runtime, read at suspend/resume time, so the
three modes can be compared without a reboot):

  * `ath12k_mhi_stop(is_suspend=true)` -> `ATH12K_MHI_POWER_OFF` (full power down, device reset)
    instead of `ATH12K_MHI_POWER_OFF_KEEP_DEV`
  * `ath12k_pci_power_up()` on a resume does `ath12k_pci_sw_reset()` instead of skipping the reset
  * `ath12k_core_resume_early()` always re-attaches through the crash-recovery path, which downloads
    the firmware again when the device has come back in SBL

Modes:
  `a16_keep_mhi_up=1`                      -> keep the MHI link and firmware (previous behaviour, unchanged)
  `a16_keep_mhi_up=0 a16_power_cycle=0`    -> mhi_power_down_keep_dev() + re-attach to the running firmware
  `a16_keep_mhi_up=0 a16_power_cycle=1`    -> **default**: full power cycle (reset + firmware reload)

Build record
------------
Built through `BRINGUP/tools/a16-install-ath12k-resume-fix.sh build`, and only after
`BRINGUP/tools/a16-fix-build-config.sh` + `BRINGUP/tools/a16-abi-layout-gate.sh`:

  * the gate FAILED first: the tree's `.config` had drifted from the running kernel's (23 lines --
    `CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT`, `CONFIG_EXT_GROUP_SCHED`, `CONFIG_EXT_SUB_SCHED`, `CONFIG_NET_SCH_BPF`
    lost, which moves every field after `task_struct.sched_class`; `thread_pid` 1824 vs the kernel's
    2144).  This is the pahole/BTF drift the fix script exists for; it restored the options and proved
    `thread_pid = 2144`, gate then PASS.
  * a first rebuild after that produced an identical .ko ("up to date") -- the stale objects were not
    recompiled -- so the module directory was cleaned and rebuilt for real.
  * result: `ath12k.ko` sha256 `d5de83934902b36d27c3e74028a1cb8b` (previous stale build
    `7b0782704b1ec8c71100ad3ea3d8ac53`), `vermagic 7.3.0-rc3-next-20260914 ...` matching the kernel,
    `module_layout 0xe6658f7b` (kernel's `0xe6658f7b`), 346 imports, 0 without a CRC, 0 disagreeing.

Install and test
----------------
    sudo bash ~/a16.sh radiofix          # install into /lib/modules/<kver>/updates/a16/ + depmod
    # reboot, then one deliberate s2idle suspend:
    sudo bash ~/a16.sh sleep measure     # or close the lid with 'sleep lid suspend'
    sudo reload_wifi status              # then: did the radio come back on its own?

Log lines to look for in the resume:

    A16: suspend -- taking the device down for a power cycle (full MHI power down, device reset)
    A16: resume -- re-attaching the driver (power cycle: full reset + firmware reload)

Expected outcomes: the radio comes back by itself (the platform really did cut power and the driver now
re-initialises correctly), or the failure moves somewhere informative (e.g. the firmware download
failing against a device that is still powered).  Either is progress.  `radiofix revert` removes the
module; `echo 0 > /sys/module/ath12k/parameters/a16_power_cycle` switches to the keep-dev/re-attach
mode at runtime for comparison.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
@@ -53,6 +53,29 @@
 static bool a16_keep_mhi_up;
 module_param(a16_keep_mhi_up, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_keep_mhi_up, "A16: keep the MHI link (and the device's firmware) up across a suspend");
+
+/* A16: a16_power_cycle -- prepare for power loss, i.e. treat a system suspend as a power cycle.
+ *
+ * Upstream documents this platform behaviour: "[RFC PATCH 0/1] soc: qcom: rpmh-rsc: Register
+ * s2idle_ops to indicate s2ram behavior in s2idle" -- "the quirky platform behavior of mimicking
+ * s2ram in s2idle for older Qcom SoCs ... device drivers ... need to prepare for the possible power
+ * loss during system suspend by shutting down or resetting the devices."
+ *
+ * Measured on this machine (glymur / Zenbook A16 UX3607OA, 2026-10-02): after a suspend the WiFi
+ * device is healthy at the PCIe level (link 8.0 GT/s, D0, driver bound, netdev present) while its
+ * firmware is dead, and a USB controller on the same resume pass fails with -110 as well.  With this
+ * set, the suspend does a full MHI power down (device reset) instead of mhi_power_down_keep_dev(),
+ * and the resume does the full cold bring-up and then reloads the firmware through the crash-recovery
+ * path -- that is, it behaves as if the device lost power, because on this machine it may have.
+ *
+ *   0 (with a16_keep_mhi_up=0): mhi_power_down_keep_dev() + re-attach to the running firmware
+ *   1 (default):               mhi_power_down() + full reset + firmware reload
+ * Writable at runtime: it is read at suspend/resume time, so the modes can be compared without a
+ * reboot.
+ */
+bool a16_power_cycle = true;
+module_param(a16_power_cycle, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_power_cycle, "A16: treat a system suspend as a power cycle (full device reset + firmware reload)");
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_fix_level, "A16: highest local fix included in this build");
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(ftm_mode, "Boots up in factory test mode");
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(ath12k_ftm_mode);
@@ -199,8 +222,13 @@
 
 	if (a16_keep_mhi_up)
 		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: suspend -- keeping the MHI link and the device's firmware up\n");
-	else
+	else {
+		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: suspend -- taking the device %s\n",
+			    a16_power_cycle ?
+			    "down for a power cycle (full MHI power down, device reset)" :
+			    "down but keeping it (mhi_power_down_keep_dev)");
 		ath12k_hif_power_down(ab, true);
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -252,8 +280,16 @@
 	 * ath12k_core_start(), which redoes the HTC/WMI handshake).  That path also completes
 	 * restart_completed, which is what ath12k_core_resume() is waiting for.
 	 */
-	if (test_and_clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_A16_KEPT_DEVICE, &ab->dev_flags)) {
-		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: resume -- device kept across the suspend; re-attaching the driver to it\n");
+	/* A16: a16_power_cycle -- the suspend took the device down, so the firmware is not running
+	 * either: re-attach the driver and let the crash-recovery path bring the device back to mission
+	 * mode, which downloads the firmware again when the device has come up in SBL after the reset.
+	 */
+	if (a16_power_cycle ||
+	    test_and_clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_A16_KEPT_DEVICE, &ab->dev_flags)) {
+		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: resume -- re-attaching the driver%s\n",
+			    a16_power_cycle ?
+			    " (power cycle: full reset + firmware reload)" :
+			    " to the device that was kept across the suspend");
 		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY, &ab->dev_flags);
 		queue_work(ab->workqueue, &ab->restart_work);
 	}
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mhi.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mhi.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mhi.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mhi.c
@@ -499,13 +499,17 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+extern bool a16_power_cycle;
+
 void ath12k_mhi_stop(struct ath12k_pci *ab_pci, bool is_suspend)
 {
 	/* During suspend we need to use mhi_power_down_keep_dev()
 	 * workaround, otherwise ath12k_core_resume() will timeout
-	 * during resume.
+	 * during resume.  A16: unless a16_power_cycle is set -- on this platform s2idle can cut
+	 * power to the endpoint, so prepare for power loss with a full power down (device reset)
+	 * instead, and let the resume do the full bring-up + firmware reload.
 	 */
-	if (is_suspend)
+	if (is_suspend && !a16_power_cycle)
 		ath12k_mhi_set_state(ab_pci, ATH12K_MHI_POWER_OFF_KEEP_DEV);
 	else
 		ath12k_mhi_set_state(ab_pci, ATH12K_MHI_POWER_OFF);
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
@@ -1454,6 +1454,8 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+extern bool a16_power_cycle;
+
 int ath12k_pci_power_up(struct ath12k_base *ab)
 {
 	struct ath12k_pci *ab_pci = ath12k_pci_priv(ab);
@@ -1478,12 +1480,16 @@
 	 * So on a resume, leave the reset line alone and let MHI re-attach to the firmware that is
 	 * still there.  Set a16_skip_global_reset_on_resume=0 to compare against the old behaviour.
 	 */
-	if (a16_skip_global_reset_on_resume &&
+	if (a16_skip_global_reset_on_resume && !a16_power_cycle &&
 	    test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_A16_RESUMING, &ab->dev_flags)) {
 		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: resume -- not resetting the device (MHI state 0x%x); the firmware should still be running\n",
 			    mhi_get_mhi_state(ab_pci->mhi_ctrl));
 		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_A16_KEPT_DEVICE, &ab->dev_flags);
 	} else {
+		/* A16: a16_power_cycle -- reset the device as a cold boot does.  The device was taken
+		 * down by the suspend (and may have lost power), so the full bring-up plus the
+		 * firmware-crash recovery path that follows is what gets the firmware back.
+		 */
 		ath12k_pci_sw_reset(ab_pci->ab, true);
 	}
 
